\section*{Conceptual Division Between Two Sub-Fields}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{\textcolor{red}{Long-run growth}}: Study the determinants of trend growth.

    \item \textbf{\textcolor{red}{Short-run business cycles}}: Study deviations from trend.
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Business cycles}: Short-term fluctuations of output around its long term level     (also called potential or natural output).  \textcolor{red}{Most recessions are brief}. Instead, the time to return to the previous peak level or the previous trend path may be quite extended. 


\end{itemize}

\textbf{Three generations:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Generation 0: \textcolor{red}{Large-Scale Macroeconometric Models}
\item First generation: basic \textcolor{red}{Real Business Cycle} models
        \item Second generation: \textcolor{red}{New Keynesian} models
        \item Third generation: \textcolor{red}{Heterogeneous-Agent} models
        \item Separate: \textcolor{red}{Search and Matching} models
    \end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Specifying a Model I}}

\begin{itemize}
    \item To describe an economy, one needs to specify:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textcolor{red}{Commodities:} what is consumed, produced, and traded.
        \item \textcolor{red}{Preferences:} what commodities households like or dislike.
        \item \textcolor{red}{Endowments:} exogenously given quantities of commodities.
        \item \textcolor{red}{Technology:} how firms transform commodities (inputs) into other commodities (outputs).
        \item \textcolor{red}{Policies:} policy instruments controlled by the government (taxes, etc.).
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\bigskip


\begin{itemize}
    \item A model typically features (at most) three types of agents. For each, we must specify decisions and constraints:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item \textcolor{red}{Households:} choose commodities to max preferences, subject to a constraint set that specifies which combination of commodities they can choose from.
        \item \textcolor{red}{Firms:} choose inputs to max (expected) profits, subject to their production plans being technologically feasible.
        \item \textcolor{red}{Government:}
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textcolor{blue}{Positive analysis:} policies taken as given, s.t.\ a budget constraint.
            \item \textcolor{blue}{Normative analysis:} policies chosen to max an objective, s.t.\ a budget constraint.
        \end{itemize}
    \end{enumerate}

    \item To obtain predictions, we must also specify:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item An \textcolor{red}{equilibrium concept}, describing how agents interact.
        \item The \textcolor{red}{information structure} available to agents when making decisions.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\section*{RBC and Monetary Neutrality}

\textbf{1. RBC Environment}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Representative household and profit-maximizing firms
    \item Perfect competition, flexible prices and wages
    \item Complete markets (no relevant financial frictions)
    \item Rational expectations, no nominal rigidities
\end{itemize}

\begin{center}
\emph{All prices adjust instantly.}
\end{center}

\medskip

\textbf{2. Monetary Policy}
Monetary policy affects nominal variables:
\[
i_t,\; M_t,\; \pi_t.
\]
Key question:
\begin{center}
Can nominal changes affect real allocations $(C, L, Y, K)$?
\end{center}

\medskip

\textbf{3. Mechanism: Relative Prices}
Economic decisions depend only on real objects:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Households: real wages
    \item Firms: real marginal costs
    \item Intertemporal choice: real interest rate
\end{itemize}

\begin{center}
\emph{Only relative prices matter.}
\end{center}

A monetary expansion implies:
\begin{itemize}
    \item All nominal prices and wages scale proportionally
    \item Fisher equation adjusts nominal interest rates
\end{itemize}

But:
\[
\text{real wage},\; \text{real interest rate},\; \text{MPC conditions}
\quad \text{unchanged}.
\]

\begin{center}
\textbf{Real allocations are invariant (classical neutrality).}
\end{center}

\medskip

\textbf{4. Why Neutrality Holds}
With fully flexible prices:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Monetary expansion raises nominal demand
    \item Prices and wages adjust one-for-one
    \item No change in real wages or labor supply
\end{itemize}

\begin{center}
\emph{No real distortions arise.}
\end{center}

\medskip

\textbf{5. Intuition: Money as a Veil}
Money only affects units of account:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Nominal values change
    \item Real tradeoffs remain unchanged
\end{itemize}
\textit{Example: converting a euro economy to dollars changes every price and wage by the exchange rate, but no one works more, produces more, or consumes more. All real allocations are identical --- only the unit of measurement changed. That's what ``money is a veil'' means.}

\medskip

\textbf{6. When Money Matters}
Neutrality fails if adjustment is imperfect:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Sticky prices or wages
    \item Information frictions
    \item Financial constraints or segmented markets
\end{itemize}

\begin{center}
\emph{Without frictions, no real effects of money.}
\end{center}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{What is Calibration?}}

\textcolor{red}{Calibration} is a method to assign values to model parameters so that the model aligns with observed empirical data. 
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Three Sources of Evidence}}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textcolor{red}{Independent micro evidence}: borrow estimates of risk aversion ($\sigma$), IES ($1/\sigma$), Frisch elasticity ($1/\varphi$) from micro studies.
    \item \textcolor{red}{Targeted long-run moments}: the procedure is: (1) select empirical targets (key moments, ratios, or facts the model should replicate), (2) map parameters to targets using steady-state conditions to express each parameter as a function of observable quantities, (3) assign values so that model-implied allocations match empirical targets. Examples:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Euler equation: $1 = \beta(1+r)$ $\Rightarrow$ average real rate pins down $\beta$
        \item Labor demand: $1-\alpha = wn/y$ $\Rightarrow$ labor share pins down $\alpha$
        \item Capital accumulation: $i/k = \delta$ $\Rightarrow$ investment-capital ratio pins down $\delta$
    \end{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{red}{Untargeted business-cycle moments}: volatility of output, consumption, hours; correlations across variables. These are \textbf{not} used to set parameters---they are used to \textbf{evaluate} the model. Data used for evaluation are distinct from those used to discipline parameters.
\end{enumerate}


\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Calibration vs.\ Structural Estimation}}

\begin{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Structural estimation}: parameters estimated from the \textbf{same} data (MLE, Bayesian, GMM/SMM). Evaluation: statistical fit, out-of-sample.
\end{itemize}

Why studying the planner problem?
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{red}{Characterize efficiency => Assess policy :} in frictionless environments, the competitive equilibrium coincides with the planner’s allocation. Deviations from the planner’s solution reveal inefficiencies and the potential scope for policy.

    \item \textcolor{red}{Simplify analysis:} the planner’s problem abstracts from prices and is typically easier to solve.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{1. Key Mechanism}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Mechanisms:
    \begin{enumerate}[nosep]
        \item \textcolor{orange}{Intratemporal}: within-period consumption-leisure trade-off. FOC: $-u_n = u_c \cdot w_t$. Work until disutility of last hour $=$ wage in utils. Pins down the \emph{level} of hours given $w_t$ and $c_t$.
        \item \textcolor{orange}{Intertemporal substitution of consumption}: Euler equation. Shift consumption across time in response to interest rate changes. Governed by IES $= 1/\sigma$. Hours adjust indirectly: the Euler tilts $c_t$, and the intratemporal FOC adjusts $n_t$ as a consequence.
    \end{enumerate}

\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{2. Model Primitives}}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{orange}{Preferences:} utility $u(c_t, n_t)$, + in consumption $c_t$,  and - in labor $n_t$. All is state dependent. 
    \item \textcolor{orange}{Technology:} production $Y_t = A_t F(k_t, n_t)$, CRS, with \textbf{stochastic TFP} $A_t$. Markov Process characterized by time invariant transition Probabilities. 
    \item \textcolor{orange}{Capital:} 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Capital stock $x_t \neq$ capital services $k_t$: the stock is what you \emph{own}, services are what you \emph{rent out}.

 \item One unit of stock produces one unit of services: $k_t \le x_t$. They both depreciate, but $k_t$ earns a rent, hence you set $k_t = x_t$.     
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 0: Setup (full problem, before simplification).}} 

\textbf{States:} $x_t(s^{t-1})$ (capital stock, endogenous, inherited from last period), $A_t(s^t)$ (exogenous productivity), $s_t$ (current shock).

\textbf{Controls:} $\{c_t(s^t),\, i_t(s^t),\, n_t(s^t),\, x_{t+1}(s^t),\, k_t(s^{t-1})\}$. Here $k_t$ is capital \emph{services} (how much of the stock $x_t$ you use in production), subject to $0 \le k_t \le x_t$. It is formally a control, but since $F_k > 0$ it is always optimal to use the entire stock: $k_t = x_t$. After simplification, $x_t$ and $k_t$ merge into a single variable $k_t$.

Note: consumption is state dependent! (umbrella on rainy day is different than umbrella in sunny day!)
\[
\max_{\{c_t(s^t),\, i_t(s^t),\, n_t(s^t),\, x_{t+1}(s^t),\, k_t(s^{t-1})\}_{t=0,\, s^t \in S^t}^{\infty}}
\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}
\sum_{s^t \in S^t}
\beta^t \pi(s^t)\,
u\!\big(c_t(s^t),\, n_t(s^t)\big)
\quad \text{s.t.} \quad
\begin{cases}
c_t(s^t) + i_t(s^t) \le A_t(s^t)\, F\!\big(k_t(s^{t-1}),\, n_t(s^t)\big) & \text{(resource)} \\
x_{t+1}(s^t) = (1-\delta)\, x_t(s^{t-1}) + i_t(s^t) & \text{(accumulation)} \\
0 \le k_t(s^{t-1}) \le x_t(s^{t-1}) & \text{(services $\le$ stock)} \\
0 \le n_t(s^t) \le 1 & \text{(labor bounds)} \\
c_t(s^t) \ge 0,\; x_{t+1}(s^t) \ge 0 & \text{(non-negativity)} \\
x_0,\; A_0,\; s_0 \text{ given}
\end{cases}
\]

\medskip
\textcolor{red}{Reducing the constraints:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{orange}{Merge resource + accumulation:} substitute $i_t = x_{t+1} - (1-\delta)x_t$ into the resource constraint.
    \item \textcolor{orange}{Capital stock = services:} one unit of stock produces one unit of services $\Rightarrow$ optimal to set $k_t = x_t$. Use $k_t$ for both.
    \item Drop $s^t$ notation: $\sum_{s^t} \pi(s^t) [\cdot] = \mathbb{E}_0[\cdot]$, where $\mathbb{E}_0$ means \textcolor{red}{expected} discounted utility taken at $t=0$. Can drop also feasibility in the max notation.
\end{itemize}

Reduced model (we will use from now on):
\[
\max_{\{c_t, \textcolor{blue}{n_t}, k_{t+1}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}} \textcolor{red}{\mathbb{E}_0} \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \beta^t u(c_t, \textcolor{blue}{n_t})
\quad \text{s.t.} \quad
\begin{cases}
c_t + k_{t+1} \le \textcolor{red}{A_t} F(k_t, \textcolor{blue}{n_t}) + (1-\delta)k_t \\
c_t \ge 0,\; k_{t+1} \ge 0,\; \textcolor{blue}{0 \le n_t \le 1} \\
k_0 \text{ given},\; \textcolor{red}{A_0 \text{ given}},\; \textcolor{red}{s_0 \text{ given}}
\end{cases}
\]

Note: since agents are identical, the planner's problem can be written in terms of a \textcolor{red}{representative agent}.

\medskip
\textcolor{orange}{Special case} (deterministic, no labour, $c_t$ substituted out):
\[
\max_{\{k_{t+1}\}} \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \beta^t u\bigl(f(k_t) - k_{t+1}\bigr) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad 0 \le k_{t+1} \le f(k_t).
\]

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{RBC: Planner's Recursive Formulation (in 'notation)}}

\[
V(\underbrace{k, \textcolor{red}{A, s}}_{\text{State vars}})
=
\max_{\underbrace{c,\, \textcolor{blue}{n},\, k'}_{\text{Control vars}}}
\left\{
u(c, \textcolor{blue}{n})
+
\beta\,
\textcolor{red}{\mathbb{E}[\textcolor{black}{V(k'}, A', s') \mid s]}
\right\} \quad \text{s.t.}
\quad
\begin{cases}
c + k' \le \underbrace{\textcolor{red}{A}\, \underbrace{F(k, \textcolor{blue}{n})}_{\text{SNGM: } f(k)} + (1-\delta)k}_{\text{NGM: } f(k)} \\[0.5em]
c \ge 0,\; k' \ge 0,\; \textcolor{blue}{0 \le n \le 1} \\[0.5em]
\textcolor{red}{A' = A(A, s')}
\end{cases}
\]

Policy functions: $c = C(k, \textcolor{red}{A, s})$, $\textcolor{blue}{n = N(k, \textcolor{red}{A, s})}$, $k' = G(k, \textcolor{red}{A, s})$.

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Key insights:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item This is a \textcolor{orange}{functional equation}: the unknown is the entire function $V(\cdot)$.
    \item The problem is \textcolor{orange}{stationary} --- preferences, technology, and constraints are time-invariant. The Bellman equation is a contraction under standard assumptions (CMT), so $V$ exists, is unique, and can be computed by value function iteration from any initial guess.
\end{itemize}

Euler derivation: see ``Euler via Envelope Theorem'' note.

\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Why can we write a Bellman equation? (Markov structure)}}

In the sequential formulation, everything depends on the \textbf{entire history} $s^t = (s_0, s_1, \ldots, s_t)$. A Bellman equation requires a \textbf{finite-dimensional state} --- a small set of variables that summarise everything you need for today's decision.

\textbf{The problem:} if productivity depends on the full history $s^t$ in some arbitrary way, there is no finite state. You'd need to carry around the entire past --- not recursive.

\textbf{The extra structure:} we need $z_t$ to be \textbf{Markov}: $\Pr(z_{t+1} \mid s^t) = \Pr(z_{t+1} \mid z_t)$. The future depends only on where you are now, not how you got there. Then $z_t$ alone is a sufficient statistic for the future.

\textbf{Example:} $\ln z_t = \rho\ln z_{t-1} + \varepsilon_t$, $\varepsilon_t \sim$ i.i.d. Knowing $z_t$ tells you the full distribution of $z_{t+1}$. State: $(k_t, z_t)$ --- finite-dimensional $\Rightarrow$ Bellman works.

\textbf{How notation changes:} sequential uses $c_t(s^t)$, sums $\sum_{s^t}\pi(s^t)[\ldots]$. Recursive uses $c = c(k, z)$, replaces history sums with $\mathbb{E}_t[\ldots \mid z_t]$.

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 0: Setup.}} \textcolor{orange}{Key difference vs.\ planner:} the resource constraint is replaced by a budget constraint; agents trade at market prices. The planner's allocation will be replicated by competitive prices (First Welfare Theorem).

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{orange}{Firm:} static, rents $k_t$ and $n_t$ at prices $r^k_t$, $w_t$, takes $A_t$ as given.
    \item \textcolor{orange}{Household:} owns capital stock $x_t$, supplies capital services $k_t \le x_t$ and labor $n_t$, chooses $c_t$, $i_t$, $x_{t+1}$. Owns firms ($\pi_t$).
    \item \textcolor{orange}{States:} $k_t(s^{t-1})$ (endogenous), $A_t(s^t)$ (exogenous), $s_t$ (current shock) --- same as planner.
    \item \textcolor{orange}{Controls:} $c_t(s^t),\; \textcolor{blue}{n_t(s^t)},\; i_t(s^t),\; x_{t+1}(s^t)$. Capital services $k_t(s^{t-1}) = x_t(s^{t-1})$ trivially (always rent out everything).
\end{itemize}

\bigskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Firm Problem (static, solved for each $s^t$).}}
\[
\max_{k_t,\, \textcolor{blue}{n_t} \ge 0}\; \pi = \textcolor{red}{A_t}\, F(k_t,\, \textcolor{blue}{n_t}) - r^k_t\, k_t - w_t\, \textcolor{blue}{n_t}
\]
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{FOCs (optimal factor demands):}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Labour:} $w_t = \textcolor{red}{A_t}\, F_n(k_t, \textcolor{blue}{n_t})$. LHS: marginal cost of one more unit of labour (the wage). RHS: marginal product of labour $\times$ productivity $A_t$.
    \item \textbf{Capital:} $r^k_t = \textcolor{red}{A_t}\, F_k(k_t, \textcolor{blue}{n_t})$. LHS: marginal cost of renting one more unit of capital (rental rate). RHS: marginal product of capital $\times$ productivity $A_t$.
\end{itemize}
\textit{Both: the firm equates the factor price (marginal cost) to its marginal revenue product. Productivity $A_t$ scales both --- when $A_t$ rises, each unit of labour and capital produces more, so the firm is willing to pay more for both factors.}

CRS $\Rightarrow$ $\pi_t = 0$ (Euler's theorem). Representative firm is WLOG. 


\bigskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Household Problem --- Full Constraints (Arrow--Debreu $s^t$ form).}}
\[
\max_{\{c_t(s^t),\, \textcolor{blue}{n_t(s^t)},\, i_t(s^t),\, x_{t+1}(s^t)\}_{t=0,\, s^t \in S^t}^{\infty}}
\textcolor{red}{\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}
\sum_{s^t \in S^t}
\beta^t \pi(s^t)}\,
u\!\big(c_t(s^t),\, \textcolor{blue}{n_t(s^t)}\big)
\quad \text{s.t.} \quad
\begin{cases}
c_t(s^t) + i_t(s^t) \le r^k_t(s^t)\, k_t(s^{t-1}) + w_t(s^t)\, \textcolor{blue}{n_t(s^t)} + \pi_t(s^t) & \text{(budget)} \\
x_{t+1}(s^t) = (1-\delta)\, x_t(s^{t-1}) + i_t(s^t) & \text{(accumulation)} \\
0 \le k_t(s^{t-1}) \le x_t(s^{t-1}) & \text{(services $\le$ stock)} \\
\textcolor{blue}{0 \le n_t(s^t) \le 1} & \text{(labor bounds)} \\
c_t(s^t) \ge 0,\; x_{t+1}(s^t) \ge 0 & \text{(non-negativity)} \\
x_0,\; \textcolor{red}{A_0},\; \textcolor{red}{s_0} \text{ given}
\end{cases}
\]

\textcolor{orange}{Reducing the constraints} (same procedure as planner):
\begin{itemize}
    \item Merge budget + accumulation: substitute $i_t = x_{t+1} - (1-\delta)x_t$.
    \item Capital stock = services: $k_t = x_t$.
    \item Zero profits: $\pi_t = 0$ (CRS).
    \item Drop $s^t$, use $\mathbb{E}_0$.
\end{itemize}\\

\textbf{Reduced Household Problem:}
\[
\max_{\{c_t, \textcolor{blue}{n_t}, k_{t+1}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}}
\textcolor{red}{\mathbb{E}_0} \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \beta^t u(c_t, \textcolor{blue}{n_t})
\quad \text{s.t.} \quad
\begin{cases}
c_t + k_{t+1} \le (r^k_t + 1 - \delta)k_t + w_t \textcolor{blue}{n_t} \\
c_t \ge 0 \\
k_{t+1} \ge 0 \\
\textcolor{blue}{0 \le n_t \le 1} \\
k_0 \text{ given}, \textcolor{red}{A_0 \text{ given,}}\ \textcolor{red}{s_0 \text{ given}}
\end{cases}
\]

\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Alternative: event-history notation (from PS2 Q3)}}

The same problem written with explicit history-contingent notation. The only primitive exogenous stochastic object is $z_t(s^t)$, where $s^t$ denotes the history of events up to $t$. All other objects ($c_t(s^t)$, $n_t(s^t)$, etc.) depend on $s^t$ through their dependence on productivity.

\textbf{Key timing distinction:} $c_t(s^t)$, $n_t(s^t)$, $k_{t+1}(s^t)$, $b_{t+1}(s^t)$ depend on $s^t$ --- chosen \emph{after} seeing the period-$t$ shock. But $k_t(s^{t-1})$ and $b_t(s^{t-1})$ depend on $s^{t-1}$, not $s^t$ --- they were chosen at $t{-}1$, \emph{before} the period-$t$ shock is realised. Capital and bonds are \textbf{predetermined}: you enter period $t$ with $k_t$ and $b_t$ already decided, then observe $z_t$ and choose $c_t, n_t, k_{t+1}, b_{t+1}$.
\begin{align*}
  \max_{\substack{c_t(s^t),\; n_t(s^t),\\ k_{t+1}(s^t),\; b_{t+1}(s^t)}}
  &\;\sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \sum_{s^t}
  \beta^t \left[
    \frac{c_t(s^t)^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma}
    - \frac{n_t(s^t)^{1+\varphi}}{1+\varphi}
  \right] \pi(s^t) \\[4pt]
  \text{s.t.}\quad
  & c_t(s^t) + b_{t+1}(s^t) + k_{t+1}(s^t) = w_t(s^t) n_t(s^t) + R_t(s^t) b_t(s^{t-1})
    + [R_t^k(s^t) + 1{-}\delta] k_t(s^{t-1})
\end{align*}
Note: $b_t(s^{t-1})$ and $k_t(s^{t-1})$ depend on $s^{t-1}$, not $s^t$ --- chosen at $t{-}1$ before the period-$t$ shock.

\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Remark: $b_t = 0$ in representative-agent models.}}

In this setting, $b_t = 0$ for all $t$. Why? All households are identical, so they all want the same side of the bond market. But bonds are in zero net supply --- no trade is possible. $R_t$ adjusts so that no one wants to trade.

\textit{This is specific to the representative-agent + zero-net-supply setup. With heterogeneous agents (e.g.\ HANK) or government debt, $b_t \neq 0$.}

\bigskip
\rule{\textwidth}{0.4pt}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Deriving the lifetime budget constraint.}} Starting from the flow budget $a_{t+1} = (1+r)a_t + y_t - c_t$:

\textbf{Step 0.} Write at $t=0$ and $t=1$:
\begin{align*}
a_1 &= (1+r)a_0 + y_0 - c_0 \\
a_2 &= (1+r)a_1 + y_1 - c_1
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 1.} Forward-fill $a_1$ into $a_2$:
\[
a_2 = (1+r)\underbrace{[(1+r)a_0 + y_0 - c_0]}_{=\,a_1} + y_1 - c_1
= (1+r)^2 a_0 + (1+r)(y_0 - c_0) + (y_1 - c_1)
\]
Divide by $(1+r)^2$:
\[
\frac{a_2}{(1+r)^2} = a_0 + \frac{y_0 - c_0}{1+r} + \frac{y_1 - c_1}{(1+r)^2}
\]

\textbf{Step 2.} Forward-fill composite $a_2$ into $a_3 = (1+r)a_2 + y_2 - c_2$:
\[
a_3 = (1+r)\underbrace{[(1+r)^2 a_0 + (1+r)(y_0 - c_0) + (y_1 - c_1)]}_{=\,a_2} + y_2 - c_2
\]
Divide by $(1+r)^3$:
\[
\frac{a_3}{(1+r)^3} = a_0 + \frac{y_0 - c_0}{1+r} + \frac{y_1 - c_1}{(1+r)^2} + \frac{y_2 - c_2}{(1+r)^3}
\]

\textbf{Step 3.} Pattern: each iteration pushes $a$ one period further, adds one discounted $(y_t - c_t)$. After $T$:
\[
\left(\frac{1}{1+r}\right)^T a_T = a_0 + \left(\frac{1}{1+r}\right) \sum_{t=0}^{T} \left(\frac{1}{1+r}\right)^t (y_t - c_t)
\]

\textbf{Step 4.} No-Ponzi-Game condition: $\lim_{T\to\infty} (1/(1+r))^T a_T = 0$. LHS vanishes, rearrange $c$ to LHS:
\[
\boxed{\left(\frac{1}{1+r}\right) \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac{1}{1+r}\right)^t c_t = a_0 + \left(\frac{1}{1+r}\right) \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac{1}{1+r}\right)^t y_t}
\]
PV of consumption $=$ initial wealth $+$ PV of income. The NPG is a constraint imposed ex ante (no Ponzi schemes). It resembles the TVC but the TVC is an optimality condition on $a^*$.

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 0: Setup:}}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{orange}{Individual state:} $k$ (endogenous, own capital).
    \item \textcolor{orange}{Aggregate states:} $K$, $\textcolor{red}{A}$, $\textcolor{red}{s}$ (exogenous to household). Determine prices:
    $w = w(K, \textcolor{red}{A}) = \textcolor{red}{A}\, F_n(K, 1)$, $\quad r^k = r^k(K, \textcolor{red}{A}) = \textcolor{red}{A}\, F_k(K, 1)$.
    \item \textcolor{orange}{Controls:} $(c, \textcolor{blue}{n}, k')$.
    \item \textcolor{orange}{Perceived law of motion:} $K' = H(K, \textcolor{red}{A})$. Taken as given by the household.
\end{itemize}

\bigskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Household Bellman Equation.}}
\[
V(k, K, \textcolor{red}{A, s}) = \max_{c,\, \textcolor{blue}{n},\, k'} \left\{ u(c, \textcolor{blue}{n}) + \beta\, \textcolor{red}{\mathbb{E}\!\left[\textcolor{black}{V(k', K',} A', s') \mid s\right]} \right\}
\quad \text{s.t.} \quad
\begin{cases}
c + k' \le (r^k(K,\textcolor{red}{A}) + 1 - \delta)k + w(K,\textcolor{red}{A})\, \textcolor{blue}{n} \\
c \ge 0 \\
k' \ge 0 \\
\textcolor{blue}{0 \le n \le 1} \\
K' = H(K, \textcolor{red}{A})
\end{cases}
\]


Policy functions: $c = C(k,K,\textcolor{red}{A})$, $\textcolor{blue}{n = N(k,K,\textcolor{red}{A})}$, $k' = G(k,K,\textcolor{red}{A})$.

\bigskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Recursive Competitive Equilibrium (RCE).}}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Policy functions solve the household Bellman.
    \item Prices satisfy firm FOCs: $w = \textcolor{red}{A}\, F_n(K,1)$, $r^k = \textcolor{red}{A}\, F_k(K,1)$.
    \item \textcolor{orange}{Consistency:} $H(K,\textcolor{red}{A}) = G(K, K, \textcolor{red}{A})$.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Part A: CRRA Utility}}

\paragraph{Q(A.1) -- Relative risk aversion.}

\textbf{Computation.}
$u_c = c^{-\sigma}$, \; $u_{cc} = -\sigma c^{-\sigma-1}$. \; $\displaystyle\mathrm{RRA}(c) \equiv -\frac{c\, u_{cc}}{u_c} = -\frac{c(-\sigma c^{-\sigma-1})}{c^{-\sigma}} = \boxed{\sigma \quad \text{for all } c > 0.}$

\textbf{What RRA measures.}
One can show that $-c \cdot u_{cc}/u_c = -d\ln u_c / d\ln c$: a ratio of log changes (i.e.\ percentage changes). So RRA $= \sigma$ means: if $c$ rises by $x$\%, marginal utility falls by $\sigma \times x$\%. Does not depend on $c$: \textbf{constant} across wealth levels.

\textbf{Constancy: what it means.}
A poor person ($c = 10$) and a rich person ($c = 100$) face a $\pm 10\%$ gamble ($\varepsilon = 0.1$, $\sigma = 2$):
\begin{center}
{\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
 & \textbf{Poor} ($c = 10$) & \textbf{Rich} ($c = 100$) & \textbf{Same?} \\
\hline
Gamble & $\pm\$1$ & $\pm\$10$ & (same \%) \\
$\pi = \tfrac{1}{2}\sigma\varepsilon^2$ & $1\%$ & $1\%$ & \textbf{Yes} \\
Would pay to avoid & $\$0.10$ & $\$1.00$ & (same \%, diff \$) \\
\end{tabular}
}
\end{center}
Both pay 1\% of their wealth to avoid the same \% gamble. That's CRRA. But for a same-\$ gamble (\$5): the poor person loses 50\% of wealth ($\pi = 25\%$), the rich loses 5\% ($\pi = 0.25\%$) --- very different. CRRA equalises \emph{proportional}, not absolute, risk aversion.

\textbf{Risk premium.}
Coin flip: $\bar{c}(1+\varepsilon)$ or $\bar{c}(1-\varepsilon)$ with equal probability. Expected value $= \bar{c}$ (fair gamble). $\pi$ = fraction of $\bar{c}$ you'd sacrifice for certainty:
\[
  \pi \approx \tfrac{1}{2}\,\sigma\,\varepsilon^2
\]
\textbf{Example}: $\bar{c} = 100$, $\varepsilon = 0.1$ (win 110 or lose 90), $\sigma = 2$: $\pi = 1\%$. You'd take a certain 99 over the gamble.
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item Independent of $\bar{c}$: rich or poor, same \% to avoid same \% gamble.
  \item Higher $\sigma$ $\Rightarrow$ higher $\pi$: $\sigma = 5$ gives $\pi = 2.5\%$.
  \item $\propto \varepsilon^2$: small gambles cost almost nothing. Why? A fair gamble has zero expected gain --- only the variance (second-order) hurts.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Q(A.2) -- Intertemporal elasticity of substitution.}

\textbf{What does the IES measure?}

RRA (Q A.1) answered: ``how averse are you to risk \emph{within} a period?'' The IES answers the \emph{time} version: ``how willing are you to shift consumption \emph{across} periods?''

Concretely: if the interest rate rises (making saving more attractive), how much do you tilt your consumption toward the future? A high IES means you tilt a lot (you're flexible). A low IES means you barely budge (you insist on a smooth path).

\textbf{Derivation.}

Lifetime utility $U = \sum \beta^t u(c_t, n_t)$. Marginal utilities:
\[
  \frac{\partial U}{\partial c_t} = \beta^t c_t^{-\sigma}, \qquad
  \frac{\partial U}{\partial c_{t+1}} = \beta^{t+1} c_{t+1}^{-\sigma}.
\]
The MRS between consumption at $t$ and $t+1$ (how many units of $c_t$ you'd give up for one unit of $c_{t+1}$):
\[
  \mathrm{MRS}_{t,t+1}
  = \frac{\partial U/\partial c_{t+1}}{\partial U/\partial c_t}
  = \beta\left(\frac{c_{t+1}}{c_t}\right)^{-\sigma}.
\]
Take logs: $\ln \mathrm{MRS} = \ln\beta - \sigma \ln(c_{t+1}/c_t)$. So $\frac{d \ln \mathrm{MRS}}{d \ln(c_{t+1}/c_t)} = -\sigma$, and:
\[
  \boxed{
  \mathrm{IES}
  = -\left(\frac{d \ln \mathrm{MRS}}{d \ln(c_{t+1}/c_t)}\right)^{-1}
  = \frac{1}{\sigma}.
  }
\]

\textbf{Interpretation.}

Large $\sigma$ $\Rightarrow$ $u(c)$ very curved $\Rightarrow$ marginal utility drops steeply $\Rightarrow$ tilting consumption across periods is costly $\Rightarrow$ small IES. Small $\sigma$ $\Rightarrow$ nearly linear $u$ $\Rightarrow$ household willingly tilts $\Rightarrow$ large IES. Extremes: $\sigma\to\infty$ gives IES $\to 0$ (perfect complements); $\sigma=1$ (log) gives IES $= 1$.

\textbf{CRRA duality.} One parameter $\sigma$ controls both RRA $= \sigma$ (risk aversion) and IES $= 1/\sigma$ (intertemporal flexibility). High $\sigma$: dislike both risk and uneven paths. This link is a limitation---Epstein--Zin preferences break it.

\bigskip
\rule{\textwidth}{0.4pt}
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Labour Supply Elasticities}}

\textbf{Utility:} $u(c_t, n_t) = \frac{c_t^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma} - \frac{n_t^{1+\varphi}}{1+\varphi}$. Within-period budget: $c_t = w_t n_t + I_t$, where $I_t \equiv R_t a_t - a_{t+1}$ (non-labour income).

\bigskip
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{1. Labour Supply Function}}

From the intratemporal FOC $n_t^{\varphi} = w_t c_t^{-\sigma}$:
\[
\boxed{\log n_t = \tfrac{1}{\varphi}\log w_t - \tfrac{\sigma}{\varphi}\log c_t}
\]
Already here you can see \textbf{two effects} of a wage increase on hours:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Direct effect} ($+1/\varphi$ from the $\log w_t$ term): higher $w_t$ makes leisure more expensive $\to$ work more. This is the \textbf{substitution effect}.
    \item \textbf{Indirect effect} (through $\log c_t$): higher $w_t$ $\to$ higher income $\to$ $c_t$ rises $\to$ $c_t^{-\sigma}$ falls $\to$ marginal utility of extra income is lower $\to$ work less. This is the \textbf{income effect}.
\end{itemize}
The net effect of $w_t$ on $n_t$ depends on \textbf{what happens to $c_t$} --- and that's what distinguishes the elasticities below.

\textbf{Comparative statics.}
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textit{Higher $w_t$, holding $c_t$ fixed}: $n_t$ rises. Each hour of work now buys more consumption, so the reward of working increases $\to$ work more. Elasticity: $\partial\log n_t/\partial\log w_t|_{c_t} = 1/\varphi$.
  \item \textit{Higher $c_t$, holding $w_t$ fixed}: $n_t$ falls. When you're already consuming a lot, $c_t^{-\sigma}$ is small---the marginal utility of extra income is low $\to$ less incentive to work. Elasticity: $\partial\log n_t/\partial\log c_t|_{w_t} = -\sigma/\varphi$.
\end{itemize}



\bigskip
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{2. Frisch Elasticity --- hold $c_t$ fixed}}

If we hold $c_t$ fixed, the $\log c_t$ term is a constant. Only the direct $\log w_t$ term moves:
\[
\boxed{\varepsilon^F = \frac{1}{\varphi}}
\]
By holding $c_t$ fixed, we \textbf{kill the income effect}. What remains is the \textbf{pure substitution effect}. Always positive.

\textit{When to use:} \textbf{temporary} wage changes --- a brief wage increase barely affects consumption (you smooth it over your lifetime), so $c_t \approx$ constant.

\textbf{Why $\lambda_t$ fixed $\iff$ $c_t$ fixed.} The Frisch elasticity is formally defined as holding the marginal utility of wealth $\lambda_t$ constant. For our utility: $\lambda_t = u_c(c_t, n_t) = c_t^{-\sigma}$. Since $c_t = \lambda_t^{-1/\sigma}$ is one-to-one, holding $\lambda_t$ fixed is the same as holding $c_t$ fixed. (This equivalence relies on separability of $u$ in $c$ and $n$.)

\bigskip
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{3. Marshallian Elasticity --- hold $I_t$ fixed, let $c_t$ adjust}}

Now let $c_t$ adjust when $w_t$ changes. Within-period budget: $c_t = w_t n_t + I_t$, where $I_t \equiv R_t a_t - a_{t+1}$ (net saving flow).

\textbf{Why hold $I_t$ fixed?} When $w_t$ changes, $c_t$ can respond through two channels: (1) within-period: more labour income, (2) intertemporal: adjust savings $a_{t+1}$, which changes $I_t$. Holding $I_t$ fixed freezes channel (2) --- we isolate the static consumption-leisure trade-off. This is appropriate for \textbf{permanent} wage changes: if $w$ is higher in \emph{every} period, there's no reason to shift resources across time, so $I_t$ doesn't change. (For temporary changes, you'd save the windfall --- $I_t$ moves --- use Frisch instead.)

Both effects are active.

\textbf{Step 1: Differentiate the labour supply.} From $\log n_t = \frac{1}{\varphi}(\log w_t - \sigma\log c_t)$, differentiate w.r.t.\ $\log w_t$:
\[
  \varepsilon_t^M \equiv \frac{d\log n_t}{d\log w_t} = \frac{1}{\varphi} - \frac{\sigma}{\varphi}\,\frac{d\log c_t}{d\log w_t}
\]
(We don't know $d\log c_t/d\log w_t$ yet --- Step 2 finds it.)

\textbf{Step 2: Differentiate the budget constraint.} $c_t = w_t n_t + I_t$ with $I_t$ fixed. Differentiate:
\[
  dc_t = n_t\,dw_t + w_t\,dn_t
\]
Divide both sides by $c_t$:
\[
  \frac{dc_t}{c_t} = \frac{n_t\,dw_t}{c_t} + \frac{w_t\,dn_t}{c_t}
\]
Now multiply and divide each term to create log changes. First term: multiply and divide by $w_t$: $\frac{n_t\,dw_t}{c_t} = \frac{w_t n_t}{c_t}\cdot\frac{dw_t}{w_t}$. Second term: multiply and divide by $n_t$: $\frac{w_t\,dn_t}{c_t} = \frac{w_t n_t}{c_t}\cdot\frac{dn_t}{n_t}$. This pulls out $w_t n_t/c_t$ and creates $dw_t/w_t = d\log w_t$, $dn_t/n_t = d\log n_t$:
\[
  \frac{dc_t}{c_t} = \frac{w_t n_t}{c_t}\,\frac{dw_t}{w_t} + \frac{w_t n_t}{c_t}\,\frac{dn_t}{n_t}
\]
Define $s_t^L \equiv w_t n_t / c_t$ (labour income share). In log changes ($d\log x = dx/x$):
\[
  d\log c_t = s_t^L\,d\log w_t + s_t^L\,d\log n_t
\]
Divide by $d\log w_t$ and note $d\log n_t / d\log w_t = \varepsilon_t^M$:
\[
  \frac{d\log c_t}{d\log w_t} = s_t^L + s_t^L\,\varepsilon_t^M = s_t^L(1 + \varepsilon_t^M)
\]

\textbf{Step 3.} Substitute and solve:
\[
\boxed{\varepsilon_t^M = \frac{\overbrace{1}^{\text{substitution}} - \overbrace{\sigma s_t^L}^{\text{income}}}{\varphi + \sigma s_t^L}}
\]
The numerator is the battle: substitution ($+1$) vs income ($-\sigma s_t^L$). If $\sigma s_t^L < 1$: substitution wins. If $\sigma s_t^L > 1$: income wins.

\textit{Why is the $1$ the substitution effect?} Set $\sigma = 0$ (getting richer doesn't change your incentive to work --- no income effect): $\varepsilon^M|_{\sigma=0} = 1/\varphi = \varepsilon^F$ (pure substitution). The $1$ is what survives; the $-\sigma s_t^L$ is what the income effect adds.

\textbf{Special case $s_t^L = 1$} (all consumption is labour income, no non-labour income): $\varepsilon_t^M = (1-\sigma)/(\varphi+\sigma)$. At $\sigma = 1$ (log utility): $\varepsilon_t^M = 0$ --- the two effects exactly cancel. A 1\% wage increase raises income by 1\%, but also raises $c_t$ by 1\%, which lowers marginal utility by exactly enough to offset the higher wage.

\bigskip
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{4. Comparison}}

\begin{center}
{\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
& \textbf{Frisch} & \textbf{Marshallian} \\
\hline
Holds fixed & $c_t$ & $I_t$ (non-labour income) \\
Effects & substitution only & substitution + income \\
Formula & $1/\varphi$ & $(1-\sigma s^L)/(\varphi+\sigma s^L)$ \\
Sign & always $> 0$ & ambiguous \\
Use for & temporary $\Delta w$ & permanent $\Delta w$ \\
\end{tabular}
}
\end{center}
$\varepsilon^F > \varepsilon^M$ always (income effect is negative).

\bigskip
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{5. Intertemporal Labour Supply}}

Write the intratemporal FOC at $t$ and $t{+}1$, take the ratio, use the Euler equation:
\[
\boxed{\log\frac{n_{t+1}}{n_t} = \frac{1}{\varphi}\!\left[\log\frac{w_{t+1}}{w_t} - \log(\beta R_{t+1})\right]}
\]
\begin{itemize}
    \item $w_{t+1}/w_t \uparrow$: shift hours to the future. \textit{``Work when the wage is high.''}
    \item $\beta R_{t+1} \uparrow$: work more \emph{today}, save, leisure tomorrow. \textit{``Earn today, let interest do the work.''}
    \item Both scaled by $1/\varphi$: large $\varphi$ $\Rightarrow$ hours barely move.
\end{itemize}

\bigskip
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{6. Why This Matters}}

RBC shocks change $w_t$ and $R_{t+1}$. Hours fluctuations $=$ optimal intertemporal substitution of leisure, governed by $1/\varphi$. This is why the model's ability to match hours volatility depends critically on $\varphi$.

\begin{itemize}

    \item In the RBC model, technology shocks drive fluctuations in wages, and thus intertemporal substitution of labor.

    \item \textcolor{orange}{Frisch Elasticity and Amplification}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item The mechanism requires a sufficiently large \textcolor{orange}{Frisch elasticity}.
        \item Micro evidence suggests relatively small values (typically below 1).
        \item With a small elasticity, labor supply responds weakly to wage changes.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textcolor{orange}{Unemployment}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item The basic RBC model has no unemployment.
        \item It cannot account for the level or cyclical fluctuations of unemployment.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textcolor{orange}{Extensive vs.\ Intensive Margin}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item In the data, most variation in labor input comes from changes in employment, not hours per worker.
        \item The basic RBC model focuses only on the intensive margin.
    \end{itemize}

    \item These limitations motivate models with indivisible labor.

\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Why Search \& Matching?}}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textcolor{red}{Walrasian labor markets} clear instantaneously: no equilibrium unemployment, no vacancies, no worker/job flows. Neither framework can explain the \textcolor{orange}{coexistence of unemployment and vacancies} --- the central empirical puzzle. Neither why they fluctuate in the cycle. 
\item \textcolor{red}{RBC models}: since agents are identical, each supplies the same hours.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Search Frictions as Micro-Foundation}}

\begin{itemize}
\item Workers spend time searching; firms post costly vacancies; matching is not instantaneous.
\item A \textcolor{red}{matching function} $m(u,v)$ captures the meeting technology.
\end{itemize}\\

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Two Key Modeling Choices}}

\begin{itemize}
\item How do workers and firms meet? \textcolor{orange}{Random matching} (vs.\ directed search).
\item How are wages set? \textcolor{orange}{Nash bargaining} (vs.\ wage posting).
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Beveridge Curve: Empirics}}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textcolor{red}{Beveridge curve}: empirical negative relationship between unemployment $u$ and vacancies $v$. One of the most stable macroeconomic relationships across countries and time.

\item \textcolor{orange}{Movements along the curve} (cyclical): in expansions, firms post more vacancies and workers find jobs easily $\Rightarrow$ low $u$, high $v$. Reversed in recessions.

\item \textcolor{orange}{Shifts of the curve} (structural): an outward shift means higher $u$ at given $v$ --- interpreted as declining matching efficiency.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Prominent Explanations for Shifts}}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Post-2008:} occupational/skill mismatch, geographic mismatch (housing lock-in), extended UI disincentives, reduced recruiting intensity.
\item \textbf{COVID era:} temporary layoffs with recall expectations, pandemic relief disincentives, Great Resignation, childcare constraints.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{1. Matching Function}}

\begin{itemize}
\item $u_t$ unemployed workers and $v_t$ posted vacancies generate matches:
\textcolor{red}{(SM-1) Matching Function:}
\[
\boxed{m_t = m(u_t, v_t)}
\tag{SM-1}
\]
\item Properties: $m$ is strictly increasing, strictly concave, CRS, and $m(0,v)=m(u,0)=0$.
\item Black-box technology similar to production fun. : maps search inputs into matches. Precise form is an empirical matter.
\end{itemize}\\

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{2. Key Rates and Market Tightness}}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textcolor{red}{Labor market tightness}: $\theta_t = v_t / u_t$.
\item \textcolor{orange}{Vacancy-filling rate}: $q_t = m(u_t,v_t)/v_t$. Expected vacancy duration: $1/q_t$.
\item \textcolor{orange}{Job-finding rate}: $p_t = m(u_t,v_t)/u_t$. Expected unemployment duration: $1/p_t$.
\end{itemize}\\

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{3. CRS Implications}}

\begin{itemize}
\item CRS $\Rightarrow$ transition rates depend only on $\theta_t$. Derivation:
\textcolor{red}{(SM-2) Vacancy-Filling Rate:}
\[
\boxed{q_t = \frac{m(u_t, v_t)}{v_t} \overset{\text{CRS,}\; \alpha=1/v_t}{=} m\!\left(\frac{u_t}{v_t},\, 1\right) = m\!\left(\frac{1}{\theta_t},\, 1\right)}
\tag{SM-2}
\]
\textcolor{red}{(SM-3) Job-Finding Rate:}
\[
\boxed{p_t = \frac{m(u_t, v_t)}{u_t} \overset{\text{CRS,}\; \alpha=1/u_t}{=} m\!\left(1,\, \frac{v_t}{u_t}\right) = m(1,\, \theta_t)}
\tag{SM-3}
\]
CRS collapses both rates into functions of the single ratio $\theta_t = v_t/u_t$ (sufficient statistic).
\item $q_t$ is \textcolor{orange}{decreasing} in $\theta_t$; \; $p_t$ is \textcolor{orange}{increasing} in $\theta_t$.
\item Key identity: \textcolor{red}{(SM-4) Rate Identity:} $p_t(\theta_t) = \theta_t\, q_t(\theta_t)$.
\end{itemize}\\

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{4. Trading Externalities and Stochastic Rationing}}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textcolor{red}{Stochastic rationing}: a vacancy remains unfilled with probability $1-q_t(\theta_t)$, a worker remains unemployed with probability $1-p_t(\theta_t)$, regardless of the wage. Rationing cannot be eliminated by wage adjustments (contrast with Walrasian allocation).
\item \textcolor{red}{Trading externalities}: an additional vacancy raises $\theta_t$, which:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{orange}{Congestion}: lowers $q_t(\theta_t)$ for other firms.
    \item \textcolor{orange}{Thick-market}: raises $p_t(\theta_t)$ for workers.
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}\\

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{5. Cobb--Douglas Specification}}

\begin{itemize}
\item Common parametric form:
\textcolor{red}{(SM-5) Cobb--Douglas Matching:}
\[
m_t = \sigma_m\, u_t^{\sigma}\, v_t^{1-\sigma}
\tag{SM-5}
\]
where $\sigma_m>0$ is matching efficiency, $\sigma \in (0,1)$ is the elasticity of matches w.r.t.\ unemployment.

\item Implied transition rates:
\textcolor{red}{(SM-6) CD Vacancy-Filling Rate:} \quad \textcolor{red}{(SM-7) CD Job-Finding Rate:}
\[
\boxed{q_t(\theta_t) = \sigma_m\, \theta_t^{-\sigma}}, \qquad \boxed{p_t(\theta_t) = \sigma_m\, \theta_t^{1-\sigma}}
\tag{SM-6, SM-7}
\]
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Primitives}}

\begin{itemize}
\item Discrete time, infinite horizon, discount factor $\beta$.
\item Measure 1 of homogeneous workers (total mass normalized to 1, so quantities $=$ rates); continuum of firms (each operates at most one job).
\item All agents are risk neutral.
\item \textcolor{orange}{Workers}: employed (earn $w_t$, no on-the-job search) or unemployed (receive $b$, search; no participation margin).
\item \textcolor{orange}{Technology}: match output $y_t$ (labor-only CRS); $y_t$ stochastic, first-order Markov, common knowledge.
\item \textcolor{orange}{Separation}: exogenous at rate $\lambda$ (worker $\to$ unemployed, firm $\to$ vacancy).
\item \textcolor{orange}{Vacancy cost}: per-period flow cost $\kappa > 0$.
\end{itemize}\\

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Worker Value Functions}}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textcolor{red}{Value of employment}:

\textcolor{red}{(SM-8) Worker Employment Value:}
\[
\boxed{W_t = w_t + \beta\left[(1-\lambda)E_t W_{t+1} + \lambda\, E_t U_{t+1}\right]}
\tag{SM-8}
\]
\item \textcolor{red}{Value of unemployment}:

\textcolor{red}{(SM-9) Worker Unemployment Value:}
\[
\boxed{U_t = b + \beta\left[p_t(\theta_t)\,E_t W_{t+1} + (1-p_t(\theta_t))\,E_t U_{t+1}\right]}
\tag{SM-9}
\]
\item $b$: flow value of unemployment (benefits, home production, leisure). $w_t$: wage (may include non-wage amenities). Time subscripts reflect dependence on the aggregate state.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Firm Value Functions}}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textcolor{red}{Value of a filled job}:

\textcolor{red}{(SM-10) Firm Filled Job Value:}
\[
\boxed{J_t = y_t - w_t + \beta\left[(1-\lambda)\,E_t J_{t+1} + \lambda\, E_t V_{t+1}\right]}
\tag{SM-10}
\]
\item \textcolor{red}{Value of a vacancy}:

\textcolor{red}{(SM-11) Firm Vacancy Value:}
\[
\boxed{V_t = -\kappa + \beta\left[q_t(\theta_t)\,E_t J_{t+1} + (1-q_t(\theta_t))\,E_t V_{t+1}\right]}
\tag{SM-11}
\]
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{1. Bargaining Set and Surplus}}

\begin{itemize}
\item A matched worker-firm pair produces $y_t$ and must agree on $w_t$ such that both prefer matching to their outside options:
\[
W_t(w_t) - U_t \ge 0 \qquad \text{and} \qquad J_t(w_t) - V_t \ge 0
\]
where $U_t$, $V_t$ do not depend on $w_t$.
\item \textcolor{red}{Total match surplus}: $\boxed{S_t = (W_t - U_t) + (J_t - V_t)}$. The wage will redistribute this surplus
\end{itemize}\\

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{2. Nash Bargaining Problem}}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textcolor{red}{Generalized Nash bargaining}:

\textcolor{red}{(SM-12) Nash Bargaining Problem:}
\[
\boxed{w_t = \arg\max \left[W_t(w_t) - U_t\right]^{\eta} \left[J_t(w_t) - V_t\right]^{1-\eta}}
\tag{SM-12}
\]
Foc -> Logs -> chain rule

\textcolor{red}{(SM-13) Nash Sharing Rule:}
\[
\boxed{\eta\,(J_t - V_t) = (1-\eta)(W_t - U_t)}
\tag{SM-13}
\]
\item Equivalently, in terms of total surplus $S_t$:
\[
W_t - U_t = \eta\, S_t, \qquad J_t - V_t = (1-\eta)\, S_t
\]
\item The worker captures fraction $\eta$ and the firm captures fraction $(1-\eta)$ of total surplus.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{4. Micro-Foundation}}

\begin{itemize}
\item Nash (1950): under some assumptions unique solution exists. The model is static --- it characterizes the outcome, not the process.
\item \textcolor{orange}{Rubinstein alternative}: alternating-offers game with unique SPE; converges to Nash solution as the time between offers $\to 0$.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Free Entry Condition}}

\begin{itemize}
\item Vacancy posting is governed by \textcolor{orange}{free entry}: firms enter until

\textcolor{red}{(SM-14) Free Entry:}
\[
\boxed{V_t = 0}
\tag{SM-14}
\]

\textcolor{red}{(SM-15) Job Creation Condition:}
\[
\boxed{\underbrace{\frac{\kappa}{q_t(\theta_t)}}_{\text{Expected hiring cost}} = \underbrace{\beta\, E_t J_{t+1}}_{\text{Expected job value}}}
\tag{SM-15}
\]
\item Intuition: firms post vacancies until the expected cost of filling a vacancy equals the expected value of a filled job.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Law of Motion for Unemployment}}

\begin{itemize}
\item Unit mass of workers: $u_t$ unemployed (search), $1-u_t$ employed (no on-the-job search, no participation margin).
\item \textcolor{red}{Unemployment dynamics}:

\textcolor{red}{(SM-16) Law of Motion for Unemployment:}
\[
\boxed{u_{t+1} - u_t = \underbrace{\lambda(1-u_t)}_{\text{Inflows (separations)}} - \underbrace{p_t(\theta_t)\,u_t}_{\text{Outflows (job finding)}}}
\tag{SM-16}
\]
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{3. Steady-State Equilibrium}}

\textcolor{orange}{Objective:} Characterize the long-run equilibrium by dropping time subscripts ($y_t=y$, $\theta_t=\theta$, $w_t=w$, $u_{t+1}=u_t=u$).

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textcolor{red}{JC curve} (job creation):

\textcolor{red}{(SM-18) JC Curve (Steady State):}
\[
\boxed{\frac{\kappa}{q(\theta)} = \frac{\beta(y-w)}{1-\beta(1-\lambda)}}
\tag{SM-18}
\]

\item \textcolor{red}{WE curve} (wage equation):

\textcolor{red}{(SM-19) WE Curve (Steady State):}
\[
\boxed{w = \eta(y + \kappa\,\theta) + (1-\eta)b}
\tag{SM-19}
\]

\item \textcolor{red}{Beveridge curve} (flow equilibrium, $u_{t+1}=u_t$):

\textcolor{red}{(SM-20) Beveridge Curve (Steady State):}
\[
\boxed{u = \frac{\lambda}{\lambda + p(\theta)}}
\tag{SM-20}
\]
\end{enumerate}

\textcolor{orange}{Solution procedure}: (SM-18) JC and (SM-19) WE are two equations in two unknowns $(w,\theta)$: their intersection determines the equilibrium. Given $\theta$, (SM-20) Beveridge curve pins down $u$.

\bigskip
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{4. Comparative Statics}}

\textcolor{orange}{Objective:} Understand the slopes of the three curves (needed for analyzing shocks and policy).

\begin{itemize}
\item \textcolor{orange}{(SM-18) JC curve} --- \textcolor{orange}{negative} $w$-$\theta$ relation:
\[
w \downarrow \;\Rightarrow\; (y-w)\uparrow \;\Rightarrow\; q(\theta)\downarrow \;\Rightarrow\; \theta\uparrow
\]
Lower wages $\to$ higher profits $\to$ more vacancy posting $\to$ tighter market. If $\kappa=0$: $y=w$ (zero-profit).

\item \textcolor{orange}{(SM-19) WE curve} --- \textcolor{orange}{positive} $w$-$\theta$ relation:
\[
\theta\uparrow \;\Rightarrow\; p(\theta)\uparrow \;\Rightarrow\; U\uparrow \;\Rightarrow\; w\uparrow
\]
Tighter market $\to$ workers find jobs faster $\to$ better outside option $\to$ higher bargained wage.

\item \textcolor{orange}{(SM-20) Beveridge curve} --- \textcolor{orange}{negative} $u$-$\theta$ (equivalently $u$-$v$) relation:
\[
v\uparrow \;\Rightarrow\; m(u,v)\uparrow \;\Rightarrow\; u\downarrow
\]
Downward-sloping, convex-to-origin locus in $(u,v)$ space.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 1: Simplify (SM-13) using (SM-14).}}

Free entry $V_t = 0$ means the firm's surplus is just $J_t$. The Nash Sharing Rule becomes:
\[
\boxed{\eta\, J_t = (1-\eta)(W_t - U_t)}
\]

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 2: Express $J_t$ and $(W_t - U_t)$ in terms of $w_t$.}}

\textcolor{orange}{$J_t$ from (SM-10):} set $V_{t+1} = 0$ (free entry persists):
\[
J_t = (y_t - w_t) + \beta(1-\lambda)\,E_t J_{t+1}
\]

\textcolor{orange}{$(W_t - U_t)$ from (SM-8) minus (SM-9):}
\[
W_t - U_t = \underbrace{(w_t - b)}_{\substack{\text{flow gain:}\\ \text{wage minus}\\ \text{outside option}}} + \beta\underbrace{(1-\lambda - p_t)}_{\substack{\text{prob. stay employed}\\ \text{minus prob. find job}\\ \text{if unemployed}}}(E_t W_{t+1} - E_t U_{t+1})
\]

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 3: Substitute into Nash and solve for $w_t$.}}
\[
w_t = \eta\, y_t + (1-\eta)\,b + \underbrace{\eta\beta(1-\lambda) E_t J_{t+1}}_{\text{from } J_t} - \underbrace{(1-\eta)\beta(1-\lambda - p_t)(E_t W_{t+1} - E_t U_{t+1})}_{\text{from } W_t - U_t}
\]
Problem: this still contains $E_t J_{t+1}$ and $E_t(W_{t+1} - U_{t+1})$.

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 4: Eliminate $E_t(W_{t+1} - U_{t+1})$ using (SM-13) at $t+1$.}}

Nash holds at $t+1$ too: $E_t(W_{t+1} - U_{t+1}) = \frac{\eta}{1-\eta} E_t J_{t+1}$. Substitute:
\[
w_t = \eta\, y_t + (1-\eta)\,b + \eta\beta\, p_t(\theta_t)\, E_t J_{t+1}
\]

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 5: Eliminate $E_t J_{t+1}$ using (SM-15) and (SM-4).}}

From (SM-15): $\beta E_t J_{t+1} = \kappa / q_t(\theta_t)$. Substitute:
\[
w_t = \eta\, y_t + (1-\eta)\,b + \eta\, p_t(\theta_t) \cdot \frac{\kappa}{q_t(\theta_t)}
\]
From (SM-4): $p_t / q_t = \theta_t$. Therefore:

\textcolor{red}{(SM-17) Wage Equation:}
\[
\boxed{w_t = \eta(y_t + \kappa\,\theta_t) + (1-\eta)b}
\tag{SM-17}
\]
\textcolor{red}{Interpretation:} worker receives fraction $\eta$ of (output $y_t$ + hiring-cost savings $\kappa\theta_t$) and fraction $(1-\eta)$ of outside option $b$. The term $\kappa\theta_t$: tighter market ($\theta_t \uparrow$) $\Rightarrow$ workers find jobs faster $\Rightarrow$ better outside option $\Rightarrow$ higher bargained wage.

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Binding vs non-binding constraints (used throughout all models):}}

The problem has inequality constraints ($\le$ and $\ge 0$). Before writing the Lagrangian, ask: which bind (hold with $=$)?
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Resource constraint} ($c_t + k_{t+1} \le \ldots$): \textcolor{red}{binds}. Preferences are monotone ($u'(c) > 0$): the agent always wants more $c$, so uses all available resources. Holds with $=$.
    \item \textbf{Non-negativity} ($c_t \ge 0$, $k_{t+1} \ge 0$): \textcolor{red}{do not bind}. Inada conditions ($\lim_{c\to 0} u'(c) = \infty$, $\lim_{k\to 0} F_k = \infty$) guarantee interior solutions --- it's never optimal to set $c_t = 0$ or $k_{t+1} = 0$.
\end{itemize}
The causal chain: Inada $\Rightarrow$ non-negativity doesn't bind $\xrightarrow{\text{comp.\ slackness}}$ its multiplier $= 0$ $\Rightarrow$ drop from Lagrangian. So we write the Lagrangian on the resource constraint alone, with $=$.


\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 1: Lagrangian.}} Using the compact $\mathbb{E}_0$ notation:


$$\mathcal{L} = \textcolor{red}{\mathbb{E}_0} \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \beta^t \Big[ u(c_t,
  \textcolor{blue}{n_t}) + \lambda_t \big( \textcolor{red}{A_t} F(k_t,
  \textcolor{blue}{n_t}) + (1-\delta)k_t - c_t - k_{t+1} \big) \Big]$$

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 2: FOCs.}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(i)] w.r.t. $c_t$: $u_c(c_t, \textcolor{blue}{n_t}) = \lambda_t$

    \item[(ii)] w.r.t. $\textcolor{blue}{n_t}$: $-u_n(c_t, n_t) = \lambda_t A_t F_n(k_t, n_t)$

    \item[(iii)] w.r.t. $k_{t+1}$: $k_{t+1}$ appears in two consecutive terms of $\sum \beta^t[\ldots]$: at $t$ (as $-k_{t+1}$) and at $t{+}1$ (inside $F(k_{t+1}, \ldots) + (1{-}\delta)k_{t+1}$). Differentiating:
    \[
    \underbrace{\beta^t \lambda_t \cdot (-1)}_{\text{from period }t} + \underbrace{\beta^{t+1} \textcolor{red}{\mathbb{E}_t}\!\big[\lambda_{t+1}(\textcolor{red}{A_{t+1}}F_k(k_{t+1},\textcolor{blue}{n_{t+1}}) + 1{-}\delta)\big]}_{\text{from period }t+1} = 0
    \]
    Divide by $\beta^t$:
    \[
    \lambda_t = \beta\,\textcolor{red}{\mathbb{E}_t}\!\big[\lambda_{t+1}(\textcolor{red}{A_{t+1}}F_k(k_{t+1},\textcolor{blue}{n_{t+1}}) + 1{-}\delta)\big]
    \]
\end{itemize}

\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 3: Optimality condition for consumption}}
\textcolor{blue}{Intertemporal (Euler, from i+iii):}
$$
\boxed{
\underbrace{u_c(c_t, \textcolor{blue}{n_t})}_{u'(c_t)}
=
\beta \, \textcolor{red}{\mathbb{E}_t}
\Big[
\underbrace{u_c(c_{t+1}, \textcolor{blue}{n_{t+1}})}_{u'(c_{t+1})}
\Big(
\underbrace{
\textcolor{red}{A_{t+1}}
\underbrace{F_k(k_{t+1}, \textcolor{blue}{n_{t+1}})}_{\text{SNGM} \quad f'(k_{t+1})}
+ 1-\delta
}_{\text{NGM} \quad f'(k_{t+1})}
\Big)
\Big]
}
$$

One more unit saved today costs $u_c(c_t,n_t)$ in forgone utility; it yields $[A_{t+1}F_k(k_{t+1},n_{t+1}) + 1-\delta\big]$ extra units tomorrow, each worth $\beta u'(c_{t+1}, n_{t+1})$. Optimality: equate the two.



\textcolor{orange}{Intratemporal (labor supply, from i+ii):}
\[
\boxed{-u_n(c_t,n_t) = u_c(c_t,n_t)\, \underbrace{A_t F_n(k_t,n_t)}_{w_t}}
\]
Marginal disutility of labor $=$ marginal product of labor $\times$ marginal utility of consumption.

\textcolor{green}{Simplification (same as planner):}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{orange}{Monotonicity} $\Rightarrow$ budget holds with equality.
    \item \textcolor{orange}{Inada} $\Rightarrow$ $c_t > 0$, $k_{t+1} > 0$ (interior, no CS needed).
\end{itemize}\\

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{RBC CE --- Lagrangian \& FOCs}}

The household takes prices $\{w_t, r^k_t\}$ as given. After reducing constraints ($k_t = x_t$, $\pi_t = 0$):

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Lagrangian.}}
\[
\mathcal{L} = \textcolor{red}{\mathbb{E}_0} \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \beta^t \Big[ u(c_t, \textcolor{blue}{n_t}) + \lambda_t \big((r^k_t + 1-\delta)k_t + w_t \textcolor{blue}{n_t} - c_t - k_{t+1}\big) \Big]
\]
$\lambda_t$ current-value, inside $\beta^t$ (same convention as planner).

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{FOCs.}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(i)] w.r.t.\ $c_t$: $\quad u_c(c_t, \textcolor{blue}{n_t}) = \lambda_t$
    \item[(ii)] w.r.t.\ $\textcolor{blue}{n_t}$: $\quad -u_n(c_t, \textcolor{blue}{n_t}) = \lambda_t\, w_t$ \quad \textcolor{blue}{(dropped in SNGM)}
    \item[(iii)] w.r.t.\ $k_{t+1}$: $\quad \lambda_t = \beta\, \textcolor{red}{\mathbb{E}_t}\big\{ \lambda_{t+1} (r^k_{t+1} + 1-\delta) \big\}$
\end{itemize}\\

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Optimality conditions.}} Substitute (i) and firm FOCs ($w_t = \textcolor{red}{A_t} F_n$, $r^k_t = \textcolor{red}{A_t} F_k$):

\textcolor{orange}{Intratemporal (i+ii):}
\[
\boxed{-u_n(c_t, \textcolor{blue}{n_t}) = u_c(c_t, \textcolor{blue}{n_t})\, \textcolor{red}{A_t}\, F_n(k_t, \textcolor{blue}{n_t})}
\]
\textcolor{orange}{Intertemporal (Euler, i+iii):}
\[
\boxed{u_c(c_t, \textcolor{blue}{n_t}) = \beta\, \textcolor{red}{\mathbb{E}_t} \Big\{ u_c(c_{t+1}, \textcolor{blue}{n_{t+1}}) \big[\textcolor{red}{A_{t+1}} F_k(k_{t+1}, \textcolor{blue}{n_{t+1}}) + 1-\delta\big] \Big\}}
\]

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Equilibrium Definition}}

A \textcolor{red}{competitive equilibrium} consists of:

Prices $\{w_t, r^k_t\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$, household allocations $\{c_t, n_t, i_t, x_{t+1}, k_t^{s}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$, firm allocations $\{y_t, k_t^{d}, n_t^{d}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$, such that:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Given prices, the household allocation solves the \textcolor{red}{household's problem}.
  \item Given prices, the firm allocation solves the \textcolor{red}{firm's problem}.
  \item \textcolor{red}{Markets clear} for all $t$:
\end{enumerate}
\[
y_t = c_t + i_t
\qquad \text{(goods market)}
, \quad
n_t^{d} = n_t^{s}
\qquad \text{(labor)}
, \quad
k_t^{d} = k_t^{s}
\qquad \text{(capital services)}
\]

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Solving the Model: From Optimality Conditions to Solutions}}

However we derived the optimality conditions (planner or CE, sequential or recursive), we arrive at the \textbf{same} system --- the necessary conditions any solution must satisfy.

\textbf{Unknowns:} $\{c_t, k_{t+1}\}_{t=0}^{T}$. Think finite horizon $T$: $c_0, \ldots, c_T$ ($T{+}1$) and $k_1, \ldots, k_{T+1}$ ($T{+}1$) $= 2(T{+}1)$ unknowns.

\textbf{Equations:}
\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
    \item \textbf{Euler equation} at $t = 0, \ldots, T{-}1$: $c_t^{-\sigma} = \beta c_{t+1}^{-\sigma}[\alpha k_{t+1}^{\alpha-1} + 1{-}\delta]$ \hfill ($T$ equations)
    \item \textbf{Resource constraint} at $t = 0, \ldots, T$: $c_t + k_{t+1} = k_t^{\alpha} + (1{-}\delta)k_t$ \hfill ($T{+}1$ equations)
\end{enumerate}
The RC at $t = 0$ uses $k_0$ (given) --- that is the \textbf{initial condition}. Total so far: $T + (T{+}1) = 2T{+}1$.

\textbf{Terminal condition (TVC):} $\lim_{T\to\infty} \beta^T c_T^{-\sigma} k_{T+1} = 0$ \hfill ($+1$ equation)

\textbf{Total:} $2T{+}1 + 1 = 2(T{+}1)$ equations for $2(T{+}1)$ unknowns. Then let $T \to \infty$.

\textbf{Steady state} (needed for log-linearization):
\[
\text{From EE:}\quad \boxed{\bar{k} = \left(\frac{\alpha\beta}{1-\beta(1-\delta)}\right)^{\!\frac{1}{1-\alpha}}} \qquad \text{From RC:}\quad \boxed{\bar{c} = \bar{k}^{\alpha} - \delta\bar{k}}
\]

Log-linearization uses all four (Euler + RC linearized, boundary conditions pin down the path). Guess-and-verify only needs Euler + RC: guess policy functions $c = f(k)$, $k' = g(k)$ and check they satisfy both for any $k$ --- TVC is automatically satisfied.

\bigskip
\rule{\textwidth}{0.4pt}
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Method 1: Log-linearization}}

\paragraph{Step 0: The two equations.}

First of all, notice we will have to approximate also the RC because it is non linear as well.

\textbf{A. Resource constraint (RC):}
\[
  c_t + k_{t+1} = k_t^{\alpha} + (1-\delta)\,k_t
  \tag{RC}
\]

\textbf{B. Euler equation (EE):}
\[
  c_t^{-\sigma} = \beta\,c_{t+1}^{-\sigma}\!\left(\alpha k_{t+1}^{\alpha-1} + 1 - \delta\right)
  \tag{EE}
\]

\paragraph{Step 1: Steady states.}

In steady state all variables are constant: $c_t=c_{t+1}=\bar{c}$, $k_t=k_{t+1}=\bar{k}$.

\textbf{A. RC in steady state} (no simplification):
\[
  \bar{c} + \bar{k} = \bar{k}^{\alpha} + (1-\delta)\,\bar{k}
  \tag{SS-RC}
\]

\textbf{B. EE in steady state} (cancel $\bar{c}^{-\sigma}$):
\[
  \bar{c}^{-\sigma} = \beta\,\bar{c}^{-\sigma}\!\left(\alpha\bar{k}^{\alpha-1} + 1 - \delta\right)
  \;\;\Longrightarrow\;\;
  1 = \beta\!\left(\alpha\bar{k}^{\alpha-1} + 1 - \delta\right)
  \tag{SS-EE}
\]

\paragraph{Step 2: Log-deviation substitution.}

Define $\hat{x}_t \equiv \ln x_t - \ln\bar{x}$, so $x_t = \bar{x}\,e^{\hat{x}_t}$. Substitute into both equations with \emph{no} simplifications.

\textbf{A. RC in log-deviations:}
\[
  \bar{c}\,e^{\hat{c}_t} + \bar{k}\,e^{\hat{k}_{t+1}}
  = \bar{k}^{\alpha}\,e^{\alpha\hat{k}_t} + (1-\delta)\,\bar{k}\,e^{\hat{k}_t}
  \tag{RC-exp}
\]

\textbf{B. EE in log-deviations.} Substitute, then cancel $\bar{c}^{-\sigma}$ and distribute $e^{-\sigma\hat{c}_{t+1}}$ into the sum so that each term has a \emph{single} exponential:
\[
  \bar{c}^{-\sigma}\,e^{-\sigma\hat{c}_t}
  = \beta\,\bar{c}^{-\sigma}\,e^{-\sigma\hat{c}_{t+1}}
  \!\left(\alpha\bar{k}^{\alpha-1}\,e^{(\alpha-1)\hat{k}_{t+1}} + 1 - \delta\right)
\]
\[
  \Longrightarrow\quad
  e^{-\sigma\hat{c}_t}
  = \beta\,\alpha\bar{k}^{\alpha-1}\,e^{(\alpha-1)\hat{k}_{t+1} - \sigma\hat{c}_{t+1}}
  + \beta(1-\delta)\,e^{-\sigma\hat{c}_{t+1}}
  \tag{EE-exp}
\]

\paragraph{Step 3: First-order Taylor approximation ($e^z \approx 1+z$).}

\textbf{A. RC --- Taylor:}
\[
  \bar{c}\,(1 + \hat{c}_t) + \bar{k}\,(1 + \hat{k}_{t+1})
  = \bar{k}^{\alpha}(1 + \alpha\hat{k}_t) + (1-\delta)\,\bar{k}\,(1 + \hat{k}_t)
  \tag{RC-lin-raw}
\]

\textbf{B. EE --- Taylor.} Apply $e^z \approx 1+z$ to each exponential in (EE-exp):
\[
  1 - \sigma\hat{c}_t
  = \beta\,\alpha\bar{k}^{\alpha-1}\bigl[1 + (\alpha-1)\hat{k}_{t+1} - \sigma\hat{c}_{t+1}\bigr]
  + \beta(1-\delta)\bigl[1 - \sigma\hat{c}_{t+1}\bigr]
  \tag{EE-lin-raw}
\]

\paragraph{Step 4: Expand and substitute steady states.}
Key notes: 1) no constants will remain, and 2) use SS-RC for the RC simplification, and SS-EE for the EE simplification.

\textbf{A. RC --- final.} Expand (RC-lin-raw):
\[
  \underbrace{\bar{c} + \bar{k}}_{\text{constants}} + \bar{c}\,\hat{c}_t + \bar{k}\,\hat{k}_{t+1}
  = \underbrace{\bar{k}^{\alpha} + (1-\delta)\bar{k}}_{\text{constants}} + \bigl(\alpha\bar{k}^{\alpha} + (1-\delta)\bar{k}\bigr)\hat{k}_t.
\]
By (SS-RC), $\bar{c}+\bar{k} = \bar{k}^{\alpha}+(1-\delta)\bar{k}$, so the constants cancel:
\[
  \boxed{\bar{c}\,\hat{c}_t + \bar{k}\,\hat{k}_{t+1} = \bigl(\alpha\bar{k}^{\alpha} + (1-\delta)\bar{k}\bigr)\hat{k}_t}
  \tag{LL-RC}
\]

\textbf{B. EE --- final.} Expand all products and identify feasible SS substitutions:
\[
  1 - \sigma\hat{c}_t
  = \underbrace{\beta\bigl[\alpha\bar{k}^{\alpha-1} + 1 - \delta\bigr]}_{=\,1\;\text{by (SS-EE)}}
  + \beta\,\alpha(\alpha{-}1)\bar{k}^{\alpha-1}\hat{k}_{t+1}
  - \underbrace{\beta\bigl[\alpha\bar{k}^{\alpha-1} + 1 - \delta\bigr]}_{=\,1\;\text{by (SS-EE)}}\sigma\hat{c}_{t+1}.
\]
By (SS-EE), the constant $1$ cancels and the coefficient of $\sigma\hat{c}_{t+1}$ is $1$:
\[
  \sigma(\hat{c}_{t+1} - \hat{c}_t) = \beta\,\alpha(\alpha-1)\bar{k}^{\alpha-1}\,\hat{k}_{t+1}.
\]
Use (SS-EE) again ($\bar{k}$ can be further reduced): $\beta\alpha\bar{k}^{\alpha-1} = 1 - \beta(1-\delta)$, giving:
\[
  \boxed{\sigma(\hat{c}_{t+1} - \hat{c}_t) = (\alpha-1)\bigl[1 - \beta(1-\delta)\bigr]\hat{k}_{t+1}}
  \tag{LL-EE}
\]

\paragraph{Why log-linearize?}
The original system is nonlinear ($c_t^{-\sigma}$, $k_t^{\alpha}$, \ldots) and has no closed-form solution. Log-linearization replaces it with a \textbf{linear} system in percentage deviations $(\hat{c}_t,\hat{k}_t)$, solvable by standard eigenvalue methods. The approximation is accurate for small perturbations---the regime relevant for business-cycle analysis. Using logs ensures deviations are in comparable percentage units.

\paragraph{After log-linearization.} Write LL-RC and LL-EE as a system of linear difference equations, then solve (difficult, not needed for this course).

\paragraph{More practice.} See the Appendix for log-linearization exercises covering all equation types: multiplicative (divide by SS, take logs), additive (substitute $e^{\hat{x}}$, Taylor, cancel SS), CES (raise to power $\rho$ first), log-linear (subtract SS), and with expectations.


\rule{\textwidth}{0.4pt}
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Method 2: Guess and verify}}

Instead of linearizing, \textbf{guess} functional forms for the policy functions and check they satisfy the original (nonlinear) system. Works only in special cases (e.g.\ $\sigma = 1$, $\delta = 1$). See next snippet.

